spec: specs/optics-outside.md (stage 2)

why: stage 1 landed the arc's shape — one declaration, several
  interpreters, one of which DESCRIBES. Looking for the next real
  consumer found the best one in the tree: a tool is declared THREE
  times today. `BoardTools` writes its JSON Schema by hand
  (`schema("text" -> "string", ...)`), then a separate
  `Map[String, ToolCall => String]` re-reads those same field names
  as string literals (`str(c, "text")`), with its own error prose.
  The two structures are keyed by the same tool name written twice,
  so a tool can be declared and undispatched or dispatched and
  undeclared — the exact property `Router.describe` was built to make
  impossible. And `ToolSpec.apply[A]`/`ToolSpec.args[A]`, which
  derive both from ONE `Schema[A]`, already exist and are unused by
  these callers.

what: `Tool[A]` — name, description, argument type, and what it does.
  `Toolbox` gives `specs` and `table` from the same values, so the
  existing seam (`Map[String, ToolCall => String]`, taken by
  Mcp.Server, Handlers.tools and Stepper) is unchanged. Convert
  BoardTools and StateMcp.

risk: the derived schema is not byte-identical to the hand-written
  one — it adds `required`, which the hand-written one never
  declared. That is prompt text (jsonschema-render-is-prompt-text),
  so TestEvalJournal and the demo suites run before the gate.

next: spec commit, Tool.scala, tests, convert the two callers, docs
